    <statement><p>The position of a particle moving along the <m>x</m>-axis is given
by <m>s(t) = 2t^{2}+4</m>.  Use difference quotients to find the
velocity <m>v(t)</m> and acceleration <m>a(t)</m>, filling in the following
expressions as you do so:</p>
<p><m>v(t) = \lim\limits_{h\to0}</m>
[ <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/> / h ] = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><m>a(t) = \lim\limits_{h\to0}</m>
[ <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0003"/> / h ] = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2\mathopen{}\left(t+h\right)^{2}+4-\left(2t^{2}+4\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4t</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4\mathopen{}\left(t+h\right)-4t</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>4</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>v(t) = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{2\mathopen{}\left(t+h\right)^{2}+4 - 2t^{2}+4}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{2t^{2}+4th+2h^{2}+4-2t^{2}-4}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} 4t+2h = 4 t</me>
and
<me>v(t) = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{4\mathopen{}\left(t+h\right) - 4t}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{4t+4h-4t}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} 4 = 4.</me>



</solution>
